I’ll inspect the pattern, metadata, and reusable refinement utilities first, then build a phase inventory and refine it residual-first in GSAS-II. I’ll keep alternate structures on a watch list until the common profile is stable, and I’ll verify the exported CIFs, fractions, profile, and saved project agree exactly.

The chemistry points first to NiWO₄, with residual NiO/WO₃ and possible tungsten-oxide polymorphs as the main alternatives—not assumptions to bake into the fit. I’m now extracting peak positions and checking the locally available COD/GSAS-II interfaces so the inventory is driven by unique reflection families and physical structure records.

The peak inventory is already coherent for three phases: wolframite NiWO₄ (e.g. 19.27°, 23.96°, 24.92°, split 30.85/31.03°), monoclinic room-temperature WO₃ (the diagnostic 23.14/23.56/24.38° triplet), and rocksalt NiO (37.33/43.42/63.07/75.56/79.68°). The high-pressure WO₃ record fails that triplet, while two independent wolframite analogues support the same P2/c topology; I’m carrying the three-phase inventory into refinement and will re-test residuals afterward.

The clean common-profile baseline converged reproducibly at Rwp 5.96% with positive scales and provisional fractions near 60.0% NiWO₄, 29.8% WO₃, and 10.2% NiO. The cells moved to physically expected values, and U/W/X stayed positive and interior. I’m now checking the actual low/mid/high-angle peak shapes and residual families; only supported size/strain, asymmetry, or texture terms will survive that test.

The nuisance tests are decisive: omitting the angular correction worsens Rwp to 6.79%; zero and specimen shift are statistically equivalent, with zero marginally better (5.959% vs 5.960%). Refining polarization is strongly supported and physically interior (0.905), lowering Rwp to 5.436%. SH/L adds almost nothing and is 97% correlated with zero, while roughness parameters are 99.9% mutually correlated, so I’m pruning both rather than treating them as intensity surrogates.

Phase-width trials did not justify extra broadening: NiWO₄ size/strain collapsed to the neutral boundary, and WO₃/NiO terms improved Rwp by only ~0.001% with 29–255% relative uncertainty. I’m retaining the clean common profile. Next I’m challenging preferred orientation across symmetry-distinct axial and diagonal axes for each phase, looking for one axis that improves several independent reflection families—not just one intense peak.

The texture screen found small but reproducible orientation effects for NiWO₄ and WO₃; NiO texture is unsupported and scale-correlated. More importantly, the WO₃ residual triplet exposes a source-record issue: the old X-ray record underpredicts the 020 intensity, while the independent neutron record predicts the observed near-equal 002/020/200 triplet. I’m revisiting that phase source—and the alternate FeWO₄-derived wolframite parent—under the same background/profile/correction model before accepting any texture.

The source-record cross-check is still refining all four combinations from scratch (Zn- vs Fe-derived wolframite; old X-ray vs neutron WO₃), including save/reopen replay. This is the most important model-selection step because it distinguishes genuine texture from incorrect structure-factor intensities.

The source comparison resolved the model: replacing the old WO₃ record with the room-temperature neutron structure drops Rwp from 5.436% to 4.418%, and the FeWO₄-derived P2/c parent further improves it to 4.279% while retaining sensible Ni–O/W–O geometry. The selected no-texture fractions are now ~59.4% NiWO₄, 30.7% WO₃, and 9.9% NiO. I’m rebuilding the phase-profile/texture tests on this corrected structural baseline.

On the corrected structures, the phase-shape tests are cleaner: only NiWO₄ isotropic microstrain is supported (199±34 µstrain; Rwp 4.270%); WO₃ and NiO width terms hit neutral bounds. Texture is axis-selective: NiWO₄ [101] and WO₃ [110] are the only materially improving directions, while NiO texture is weak/inconsistent across its six lines. I’m now combining just those complementary terms and checking replay stability and geometry.

Heavy-atom coordinate refinement is strongly supported for NiWO₄: refining only Ni/W y positions lowers Rwp from 4.200% to 3.636%, with small uncertainties and modest shifts. WO₃ W-coordinate changes are minor and highly correlated, so they are unlikely to be defensible. I’m checking the resulting bond geometry now; the restrained oxygen trial needs a GSAS-II restraint-tree initialization fix before it can be judged.

The refinement has converged on three crystalline phases: wolframite NiWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, and rocksalt NiO. I’m now freezing the conservative structural model—refined heavy-atom coordinates for NiWO₄, analogue-supported oxygen positions—then replaying it from the saved project and exporting every deliverable from that same final state.

The accepted nuisance model is deliberately small: six background coefficients, one zero correction, common nonnegative U/W/X broadening, polarization, NiWO₄ isotropic microstrain, and only two supported preferred-orientation terms. Size, roughness, SH/L asymmetry, NiO texture, and WO₃ coordinate refinements were rejected as neutral, correlated, or boundary-seeking.

The disk replay is stable (maximum shift/esd fell from 0.006 to 0.001). The synchronized final state gives 59.626 wt% NiWO₄, 30.634 wt% WO₃, and 9.740 wt% NiO, with Rwp 3.636% over 8,301 points. I’m checking CIF chemistry/geometry and byte-for-byte profile/project agreement before writing the uncertainty assessment.

Validation confirms all three CIFs parse with the intended reduced formulas, no short contacts are present, the fractions sum to unity, and every exported profile value and fraction matches `final.gpx` exactly. The principal limitation is structural rather than phase identification: oxygen coordinates are reference-supported, not independently resolved by this laboratory pattern.

